  "position": 120,
  "seoTitle": "Full Adder: Circuit, Truth Table and Implementation",
  "seoDescription": "How a full adder works: the eight-row truth table, building one from two half adders, and how chaining them makes a ripple-carry adder.",
  "keywords": [
    "full adder",
    "full adder truth table",
    "full adder circuit",
    "carry in carry out"
  ],
  "body": [
    {
      "id": "p-intro",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "A "
        },
        {
          "text": "full adder",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " adds three bits: two operand bits and a carry from the previous column. It is the building block every multi-bit adder is made of."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-why",
      "text": "Why three inputs",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "p-why",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "When you add binary numbers column by column, every column except the rightmost can receive a carry. A "
        },
        {
          "text": "half adder",
          "marks": [
            {
              "href": "/tutorials/digital-logic/half-adder-implementation",
              "kind": "link"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " has no input for that, so it can only handle the first column. The full adder adds a "
        },
        {
          "text": "CIN",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "code"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " input to fix exactly that."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-truth",
      "text": "Truth table",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "t-truth",
      "rows": [
        [
          "0",
          "0",
          "0",
          "0",
          "0"
        ],
        [
          "0",
          "1",
          "0",
          "1",
          "0"
        ],
        [
          "1",
